Threat-Informed Design Thinking

This decision reduces the risk of:

-unauthorised users editing repair requests
-normal users changing maintenance progress
-accidental or malicious manipulation of repair history
-anonymous users accessing protected workflows

The highest-risk actions are protected behind staff/maintenance staff checks.

Consequences

Positive
-introduces clear authorisation rules
-supports role-based access
-improves security
-supports meaningful permission tests
-keeps permission logic reusable
-matches Django class-based view architecture
Negative
-requires group setup in Django admin
-permissions must be tested carefully
-incorrect group assignment can block valid users

Testing Strategy

Permission tests should verify:

-anonymous users are redirected to login
-logged-in users can view repair requests
-logged-in users can create repair requests
-normal users cannot edit repair requests
-normal users cannot add maintenance updates
-Maintenance Staff users can edit repair requests
-Maintenance Staff users can add maintenance updates

Final Outcome

Permission boundaries improved the realism and security of the system. The design now separates normal user actions from staff-level repair management actions, supporting the Authentication and Authorisation Architecture rubric.
